WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:05.490
&gt;&gt; Well, I have a fun job, but I'm not going to talk about

00:00:05.309 --> 00:00:06.309
the fun parts.

00:00:06.309 --> 00:00:09.559
I'm going to talk about the problems that we've had -- about

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:10.599
the hard parts.

00:00:10.619 --> 00:00:15.279
I want to start with two questions.

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:20.379
The first question is, Why do successful companies fail?

00:00:20.399 --> 00:00:26.210
Now, I grew up around this whole industry of computers, and

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.160
-- as it went through this remarkable revolution -- and saw

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:31.449
a lot of companies come and go.

00:00:31.469 --> 00:00:34.519
But there were two that stand out in my mind.

00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:39.439
One was Evans &amp; Sutherland, which is located in Salt Lake.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.389
This was one of the pioneering companies in computer

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:44.009
graphics, and they had the lead.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.169
They had more knowledge and expertise than anybody else.

00:00:47.189 --> 00:00:51.709
And they were in the best position to take that knowledge

00:00:51.729 --> 00:00:56.589
and go to the next level as a company.

00:00:56.609 --> 00:01:02.779
But they made some decisions in which they lost that lead.

00:01:02.799 --> 00:01:05.799
And a new company leapt to the front and that was Silicon

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.879
Graphics, founded by Jim Clark who incidentally used to work

00:01:09.899 --> 00:01:10.899
for Evans &amp; Sutherland.

00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:18.930
And SGI had this remarkable run.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.290
They built the graphics machines that was used throughout

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.750
the world for a variety of visualization and graphics

00:01:25.769 --> 00:01:26.769
techniques.

00:01:26.769 --> 00:01:30.170
A lot of software was built on it.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:35.120
The revolution in computer graphics in the entertainment

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.689
industry was built on their machines.

00:01:37.709 --> 00:01:39.640
They had everything.

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:43.299
But they made two serious mistakes.

00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:46.379
And the things about these serious mistakes was, there were

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:49.399
several people on the outside that took a look at that and

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:51.149
say, "That's a really bad idea.

00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:53.489
You shouldn't do that."

00:01:53.509 --> 00:01:57.359
Nevertheless, they did it, and they lost the lead.

00:01:57.379 --> 00:01:59.429
They lost the lead to Nvidia.

00:01:59.449 --> 00:02:04.769
Incidentally, made up of former SGI employees.

00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:06.289
And they did some remarkable things.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:10.669
And they, with ATA, are still out in the lead.

00:02:10.689 --> 00:02:13.689
But the company that had that lead was SGI and all the

00:02:13.689 --> 00:02:16.039
knowledge.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.239
Now, the people had said they're making the wrong decision.

00:02:20.259 --> 00:02:24.349
In some ways, they're like Cassandra, where you're telling

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:26.370
the truth, but the people don't listen.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:31.399
And Cassandra was cursed with being able to see the future,

00:02:31.419 --> 00:02:32.919
but not having people believe her.

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:37.699
And it felt like that kind of situation here.

00:02:37.719 --> 00:02:40.749
The reason I start with this question was, at that time,

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:44.109
while all this is going on, I'm with this struggling

00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:47.129
company, and we're trying to figure out how to make things

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.500
work.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.250
And we start off with a company.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:49.900
It's just hard.

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:54.269
And it was pretty -- a very difficult task.

00:02:54.289 --> 00:02:59.039
But I'm thinking, "If we're ever successful, how do I keep

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.560
from falling into the trap that these companies are falling

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:01.870
into?

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.809
And it's not like they get blindsided by something which

00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:06.249
catches them totally off-guard.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:07.769
This stuff is really pretty obvious.

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:10.889
They're missing the obvious.

00:03:10.909 --> 00:03:14.919
The second question relates to a dinner that I had with a

00:03:14.939 --> 00:03:18.469
studio executive -- the head of a studio -- a couple years

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:19.879
ago.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:22.979
And he said at that -- actually, it was a lunch -- he said

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.239
at that lunch, "Our central problem is not finding good

00:03:27.259 --> 00:03:28.269
people.

00:03:28.289 --> 00:03:31.769
It's finding good ideas."

00:03:31.789 --> 00:03:34.599
So that was an interesting expression.

00:03:34.619 --> 00:03:38.839
So as I gave talks for the next while after that, I would

00:03:38.859 --> 00:03:41.949
pose that question to the audience and ask them whether or

00:03:41.969 --> 00:03:43.219
not they agreed with him.

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:47.139
And what I found was that, in every case, the audience was

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:48.269
split 50-50.

00:03:48.289 --> 00:03:50.039
It's really pretty astonishing.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.509
Didn't matter whether they were students, experienced

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:57.439
business executives, artists, high school teachers -- they

00:03:57.459 --> 00:03:58.869
all split.

00:03:58.889 --> 00:04:01.389
Some thought it was right; half thought it was wrong.

00:04:00.979 --> 00:04:04.849
Well, let me come back to that second question later.

00:04:04.869 --> 00:04:08.649
So now, let me step into the story, which is, in '91, when

00:04:08.669 --> 00:04:11.589
our company was about five years old and went through the

00:04:11.609 --> 00:04:15.219
various hells you go through when you're starting a company.

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:17.740
We finally got our chance, and we entered into a

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:21.620
relationship with Disney to make the first computer-animated

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:24.470
film, "Toy Story."

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:29.300
And at that time, we got a few things right.

00:04:29.319 --> 00:04:32.069
We did have a culture where the artists and the technical

00:04:31.829 --> 00:04:34.310
people were peers with each other.

00:04:34.329 --> 00:04:35.879
They socialized with each other.

00:04:35.899 --> 00:04:38.870
They each thought the other was world class.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:41.439
The compensation structure was the same.

00:04:41.459 --> 00:04:42.709
They were allowed to intermarry.

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:50.480
[laughter] If there are problems -- and there were always

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.420
problems -- people felt comfortable about coming in and

00:04:53.439 --> 00:04:54.439
expressing their problems.

00:04:54.439 --> 00:04:57.439
You couldn't fix every problem, but it's important to hear

00:04:56.479 --> 00:04:57.479
them.

00:04:57.479 --> 00:05:02.810
The other thing we had was something which we called the "

00:05:02.829 --> 00:05:03.970
Brain Trust."

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.790
We had a certain group of people who were very remarkable at

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.149
telling stories.

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:14.629
And part of being remarkable was, not only that they were

00:05:14.649 --> 00:05:17.699
funny and focused and good at storytelling, but they had

00:05:17.719 --> 00:05:19.600
complete trust in each other.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.600
And they were very often -- what you might call 'brutally

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.370
honest' -- except for them, they didn't think of it as '

00:05:25.669 --> 00:05:26.669
brutally honest'.

00:05:26.669 --> 00:05:29.360
They thought of it as 'necessarily honest,' and it was

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:30.389
always taken that way.

00:05:30.409 --> 00:05:35.139
It was never a matter of ego or putting somebody down.

00:05:35.159 --> 00:05:36.819
It was always about the story.

00:05:36.839 --> 00:05:39.339
And therefore, you could say something hard, and it was

00:05:39.279 --> 00:05:40.529
taken in the right spirit.

00:05:40.529 --> 00:05:43.279
And given that kind of camaraderie in the key group of

00:05:43.279 --> 00:05:44.780
people, it is just gold.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.379
And it's an amazing group.

00:05:46.399 --> 00:05:49.149
And to this day, it's an amazing group of people.

00:05:48.629 --> 00:05:52.579
So we got that right.

00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:57.350
We had a review process that was actually a combination of

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:00.569
some things that Jonda learned from Disney, along with

00:06:00.589 --> 00:06:03.509
things that were at ILM at Lucas Film.

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:06.339
But it was unique because of that combination.

00:06:06.359 --> 00:06:11.069
But what it amounted to was that, in the process of making

00:06:11.089 --> 00:06:15.649
the film, we reviewed the material every day.

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:18.550
Now, this is counterintuitive for a lot of people.

00:06:18.569 --> 00:06:21.410
Most people -- if you can imagine this -- you can't draw

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.660
very well, but even if you can draw very well, suppose you

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.430
come in, and you've got to put together animation or

00:06:27.389 --> 00:06:32.060
drawings and show it to a famous, world-class animator.

00:06:32.079 --> 00:06:35.079
Well, you don't want to show something which is weak or

00:06:35.069 --> 00:06:36.069
poor.

00:06:36.069 --> 00:06:40.300
So you want to hold off until you get it to be right.

00:06:40.319 --> 00:06:43.800
And the trick is, actually, to stop that behavior.

00:06:43.819 --> 00:06:47.730
We show it every day when it's incomplete.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:52.060
If everybody does it every day, then you get over the

00:06:52.079 --> 00:06:53.079
embarrassment.

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:56.550
And when you get over the embarrassment, you're more

00:06:56.569 --> 00:06:58.170
creative.

00:06:58.189 --> 00:07:01.439
And that's -- as I say -- it's not obvious to people, but

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.569
starting down that path helped everything that we did.

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:05.459
Show it in its incomplete form.

00:07:05.479 --> 00:07:08.279
There's another advantage to doing that, and that is, when

00:07:08.299 --> 00:07:10.680
you're done, you're done.

00:07:10.699 --> 00:07:15.029
Now, that might seem silly, except that a lot of people --

00:07:15.049 --> 00:07:16.049
they work on something.

00:07:16.049 --> 00:07:19.549
And they want to hold it, and they want to show it, let's

00:07:19.299 --> 00:07:22.299
say, two weeks later to get done -- only, it's never 'right'

00:07:21.849 --> 00:07:22.049
.

00:07:22.049 --> 00:07:23.299
So they're not done.

00:07:23.049 --> 00:07:25.299
So you need to go through this iterative process.

00:07:25.299 --> 00:07:28.299
And the trick was to do it more frequently to change the

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:28.949
dynamics.

00:07:28.949 --> 00:07:32.199
Also, as we started up in making this film, we brought in a

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:36.449
new group of people, because we had artists, we had

00:07:36.469 --> 00:07:39.680
technical people, but we never made a movie before.

00:07:39.699 --> 00:07:42.699
So we didn't have a group of people who were really

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.219
production managers.

00:07:44.239 --> 00:07:46.989
They were the ones keeping track of things, making sure that

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:49.660
the material went from place to place.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:52.540
You got, literally, millions of things that are going on.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:57.639
There's a lot to manage, a lot to take care of.

00:07:57.659 --> 00:08:00.159
So you needed people who thought that way and were

00:07:59.909 --> 00:08:00.909
experienced with it.

00:08:00.909 --> 00:08:04.319
So this is this new group coming in from Los Angeles.

00:08:04.339 --> 00:08:10.100
So, we finish the film, and the film was a big success.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:11.870
It went way beyond what anybody thought.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:15.699
It was a great start to this new field of computer

00:08:15.719 --> 00:08:18.019
animation.

00:08:18.039 --> 00:08:19.379
And we were building company.

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:21.009
We went public at that time.

00:08:21.029 --> 00:08:24.850
We went public one week after the film opened.

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:28.120
And it was the biggest IPO of the year, I think.

00:08:28.139 --> 00:08:31.639
And we wanted people to see our product before they

00:08:31.659 --> 00:08:32.659
invested.

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:38.439
And it just went well; it was a great, whirling event.

00:08:38.459 --> 00:08:41.730
But at that time, or just prior to going public, we had to

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:43.000
start on the next film.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.000
Because you can't just have one product, and that's it.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:47.750
You've got to keep coming out with them.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:50.250
And we're very aware there's a 'second-product syndrome'

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:52.750
where you kind of overdo it in the second one, and you blow

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.000
it.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.250
So, we got somebody working on the second one.

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:58.500
And as the team started to roll off the Toy Story, we began

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.250
to put them on the new film, which is White Sock.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:04.250
And so, then we met with the artists and said, "This is

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:04.500
great.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.500
Let's move on to the next film."

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:09.000
And we met with the technical people, and we said, "Great,

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.250
we're ready for it."

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.750
And we met with the production managers.

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:15.309
They said, "What?! We don't like it here.

00:09:15.329 --> 00:09:18.309
We want to quit."

00:09:18.329 --> 00:09:24.559
So, we undercovered this major problem.

00:09:24.579 --> 00:09:28.480
They felt like they were second-class citizens -- felt like

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:30.970
people treated them second class.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:34.789
So I went around and asked people, among the artists and the

00:09:34.809 --> 00:09:36.110
technical people.

00:09:36.129 --> 00:09:38.490
And sure enough, they thought the production managers were

00:09:38.509 --> 00:09:39.509
second class.

00:09:39.509 --> 00:09:44.620
[laughter] It's all true.

00:09:44.639 --> 00:09:48.129
They thought they were impediments, that they were getting

00:09:48.149 --> 00:09:50.549
in the way of getting the job done.

00:09:50.569 --> 00:09:53.240
So we had this major disaster.

00:09:53.259 --> 00:10:01.429
So the first question is, What was the problem?

00:10:01.449 --> 00:10:07.169
And then, I want to come back to the deeper thing for me.

00:10:07.189 --> 00:10:09.929
Making a movie is a very organized activity.

00:10:09.949 --> 00:10:15.159
And so, what was happening is, because it's so organized and

00:10:15.179 --> 00:10:18.429
it's so much stuff going on, you can easily lose control of

00:10:18.179 --> 00:10:21.990
it -- that we had this very hierarchical structure in place.

00:10:22.009 --> 00:10:25.059
And the various people there wanted to make sure that all

00:10:25.079 --> 00:10:28.019
the information went through the proper channels.

00:10:28.039 --> 00:10:30.939
Because it went up the hierarchy and down the hierarchy, so

00:10:30.959 --> 00:10:33.250
that everything happened in the right place.

00:10:33.269 --> 00:10:38.309
And that whole notion of controlling that information flow

00:10:38.329 --> 00:10:42.210
pissed everybody off.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.980
And yet there was this feeling you had to maintain control.

00:10:44.579 --> 00:10:47.409
The key concept -- we realized at that time -- was that we

00:10:47.429 --> 00:10:50.220
had confused the organizational structure with the

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:54.069
communication structure, a very common thing that happens a

00:10:54.089 --> 00:10:55.460
lot of companies.

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.399
They are different.

00:10:57.419 --> 00:11:01.179
Yes, you must be organized; things must happen in order.

00:11:01.199 --> 00:11:02.980
You can lose control.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.519
But communication needs to be able to happen between anybody

00:11:07.539 --> 00:11:09.039
in the company at any time.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:13.319
It really is, you know, it's peer to peer, right?

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:15.839
It's the notion you can go to anybody else.

00:11:15.589 --> 00:11:18.379
Yes, let your managers know if you have to go anywhere.

00:11:18.399 --> 00:11:21.529
An open-door policy frequently means you can go to the head

00:11:21.549 --> 00:11:22.549
of the company.

00:11:22.549 --> 00:11:25.549
But open-door policy, to us, means anybody can go to anybody

00:11:25.149 --> 00:11:26.149
any time.

00:11:26.149 --> 00:11:27.149
Very important.

00:11:26.879 --> 00:11:29.590
And once we actually broke that down, things changed

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:31.289
dramatically.

00:11:31.309 --> 00:11:34.129
The other part of that, though -- and this is the harder

00:11:34.149 --> 00:11:37.929
thing for people to realize -- and that is that, for

00:11:37.949 --> 00:11:42.600
managers, the one thing you hate as a manager is to be

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:43.980
surprised.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.250
So you don't like to walk into a meeting and find out

00:11:46.969 --> 00:11:48.969
something for the first time in your meeting.

00:11:48.969 --> 00:11:52.469
In fact, it's kind of a sign of disrespect if you weren't

00:11:51.969 --> 00:11:53.969
told before the other people in the meeting.

00:11:53.969 --> 00:11:56.969
And if you've got everybody thinking that way, you got a

00:11:55.969 --> 00:11:56.969
problem.

00:11:56.969 --> 00:12:00.469
So one of the tricks for a manager to learn is, you don't

00:12:00.219 --> 00:12:02.219
need to be the first person to learn.

00:12:01.969 --> 00:12:02.969
Don't get upset.

00:12:02.969 --> 00:12:03.969
Get over it.

00:12:03.719 --> 00:12:04.969
It actually doesn't matter.

00:12:04.719 --> 00:12:07.219
The fact that somebody else talked about the problem ahead

00:12:07.219 --> 00:12:12.659
of time is a good thing -- not a sign of disrespect.

00:12:12.679 --> 00:12:13.679
Okay.

00:12:13.679 --> 00:12:19.199
So the other question, which was the difficult one for me

00:12:19.219 --> 00:12:21.669
was, Why had I missed this problem?

00:12:21.689 --> 00:12:25.549
We're off making this film, I was having a good time, and I'

00:12:25.569 --> 00:12:26.819
m not looking for it.

00:12:26.819 --> 00:12:29.319
I thought I had a great relationship, open door, and

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:30.110
everything like that.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:31.740
Totally missed it.

00:12:31.759 --> 00:12:36.679
Well, part of the reason was that there was a group of new

00:12:36.699 --> 00:12:38.929
people that came into an existing structure.

00:12:38.949 --> 00:12:39.949
They accepted it.

00:12:39.949 --> 00:12:47.949
They were used to reporting to producers.

00:12:47.969 --> 00:12:51.469
[pause] And if you didn't like it, well, you're only on the

00:12:51.049 --> 00:12:54.549
job for four months, you just put up with it, and you'd move

00:12:53.549 --> 00:12:54.549
on.

00:12:54.549 --> 00:12:56.799
So there was no reason for them to complain.

00:12:56.799 --> 00:12:58.049
Just put up with it.

00:12:58.049 --> 00:13:02.559
The other problem though, was that they really did like the

00:13:02.579 --> 00:13:05.789
fact that they were working on a groundbreaking film.

00:13:05.809 --> 00:13:07.809
It was very exciting to be making history.

00:13:07.809 --> 00:13:09.689
They loved that.

00:13:09.709 --> 00:13:11.759
They loved working for John Lasseter.

00:13:11.779 --> 00:13:17.809
So I realized that, because they loved a lot of things, they

00:13:17.829 --> 00:13:21.769
were willing to put up with stuff that they didn't like.

00:13:21.789 --> 00:13:24.629
And this is one of the fundamental problems, I think, with

00:13:24.649 --> 00:13:25.649
companies.

00:13:25.649 --> 00:13:32.759
And that is that "success hides problems."

00:13:32.779 --> 00:13:36.100
It happens to a lot of us in our personal lives with our

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:37.120
health.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:40.569
When we're healthy, there may be a lot of things that are

00:13:40.589 --> 00:13:43.839
bad for us, but our health lets us get away with doing stuff

00:13:43.839 --> 00:13:45.089
that's bad for us.

00:13:44.839 --> 00:13:47.839
Then years later, the logic of that time doesn't hold up,

00:13:47.589 --> 00:13:48.589
but we do that.

00:13:48.589 --> 00:13:50.339
It happens with a lot of companies.

00:13:50.339 --> 00:13:53.629
It happens with state, local, and national governments.

00:13:53.649 --> 00:13:56.649
When you're healthy, you've got the resources, you don't

00:13:56.549 --> 00:13:58.429
need to address the problems.

00:13:58.449 --> 00:14:01.949
And when I look at a lot of the companies -- I look at SGI,

00:14:01.289 --> 00:14:04.039
and so forth -- they were actually very healthy, and they're

00:14:03.649 --> 00:14:04.649
very strong.

00:14:04.649 --> 00:14:07.919
And the problems were there, but they didn't have to look at

00:14:07.939 --> 00:14:10.370
them at that time, because they lucked at success.

00:14:10.389 --> 00:14:12.159
And they were successful at that time.

00:14:12.179 --> 00:14:15.429
They let that get in the way of diving deep and finding the

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:17.429
problems.

00:14:17.449 --> 00:14:20.949
So even though I was kind of aware of this problem, I'd been

00:14:20.769 --> 00:14:21.769
caught by it too.

00:14:21.769 --> 00:14:26.329
So just being aware of it wasn't enough.

00:14:26.349 --> 00:14:31.689
So the next stage for us, while we're making Bugs Life is,

00:14:31.709 --> 00:14:33.959
we started to make a sequel to Toy Story.

00:14:33.629 --> 00:14:38.470
Now, the logic, initially, which came from Disney was, if

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:41.480
you have a successful film, you need to have follow-on

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:43.529
things to keep the characters alive.

00:14:43.549 --> 00:14:46.949
At that time it was to make direct, video sequels.

00:14:46.969 --> 00:14:51.360
So we picked a couple of animators to give them a chance to

00:14:51.379 --> 00:14:54.429
direct this picture, put together a team, started to make

00:14:54.449 --> 00:14:55.449
this film.

00:14:55.449 --> 00:14:59.659
Well, right away, we ran into a problem.

00:14:59.679 --> 00:15:03.159
And that was that one group was making a direct video

00:15:03.179 --> 00:15:06.559
sequel, which was essentially like B or C level of

00:15:06.579 --> 00:15:08.000
expectation and quality.

00:15:08.019 --> 00:15:11.399
And the other team -- the A team -- was working on A Bug's

00:15:11.419 --> 00:15:14.169
Life, where you've got the very best people and the

00:15:13.919 --> 00:15:15.919
expectation is, "We have to match Toy Story."

00:15:15.919 --> 00:15:19.250
Well, this wasn't good, we realized.

00:15:19.269 --> 00:15:22.019
You've got two different standards of quality under the same

00:15:21.959 --> 00:15:22.959
roof?

00:15:22.959 --> 00:15:27.370
We realized, "This actually isn't good for our souls to do

00:15:27.389 --> 00:15:28.389
that."

00:15:28.389 --> 00:15:31.389
We shouldn't be thinking that it's okay to be doing

00:15:31.229 --> 00:15:32.970
something which isn't great.

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:34.990
So fairly early on, we actually stopped that.

00:15:34.839 --> 00:15:38.990
We decided to make Toy Story a theatrical release.

00:15:39.009 --> 00:15:42.259
So, we decided that, Okay, we've got to go for the A

00:15:41.559 --> 00:15:42.559
quality.

00:15:42.559 --> 00:15:45.519
Now, incidentally, when you make a film -- when you start to

00:15:45.539 --> 00:15:47.289
make it -- it isn't very good.

00:15:47.039 --> 00:15:51.269
When you put the reels together and you get the story, if

00:15:51.289 --> 00:15:53.829
it's really good, you're almost done, right?

00:15:53.849 --> 00:15:56.789
But when you're starting, you're not almost done.

00:15:56.809 --> 00:16:00.309
So you've got to go through a lot of steps to get there.

00:16:00.059 --> 00:16:03.059
And what you want to see is every six months or three

00:16:03.059 --> 00:16:06.059
months, whenever you do a reel, you want to see a step

00:16:05.309 --> 00:16:06.309
forward.

00:16:06.309 --> 00:16:09.309
And sometimes you make a step back, but in general, you want

00:16:09.309 --> 00:16:10.559
to take that step forward.

00:16:10.559 --> 00:16:14.139
What we found was, this was sort of hovering around not

00:16:14.159 --> 00:16:15.360
being very good.

00:16:15.379 --> 00:16:19.990
And just lots of issues weren't right and the directors

00:16:20.009 --> 00:16:22.809
didn't get along and the producers were having problems and

00:16:22.829 --> 00:16:25.759
that wasn't a very functional team.

00:16:25.779 --> 00:16:29.279
So I went to John, who's focused on Bug's Life, and said, "

00:16:28.939 --> 00:16:30.439
Well, we got a problem here."

00:16:30.439 --> 00:16:31.689
He said, "Don't worry.

00:16:31.189 --> 00:16:32.189
Trust the process.

00:16:32.189 --> 00:16:33.689
It'll get to be better."

00:16:33.439 --> 00:16:36.029
No, it wasn't getting any better.

00:16:36.049 --> 00:16:39.589
So, in 1998, A Bug's Life is finished.

00:16:39.609 --> 00:16:43.449
And there was a worldwide tour, and John went on that tour.

00:16:43.469 --> 00:16:46.469
But he knew that, when he returned, that he then had to

00:16:46.099 --> 00:16:48.389
address the issue of Toy Story II.

00:16:48.409 --> 00:16:52.299
Now, Toy Story II, as he returned, had to be delivered in

00:16:52.319 --> 00:16:54.220
nine months, all right.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.990
So it was already a tight schedule for all they were.

00:16:56.839 --> 00:16:58.350
So he came back.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:03.409
He watched Toy Story I before he came in to work from this

00:17:03.429 --> 00:17:06.019
trip to Europe, came in to watch the reels.

00:17:06.039 --> 00:17:08.190
He walked in and said, "You're right.

00:17:08.210 --> 00:17:10.309
We've got a major problem."

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:13.329
So basically, we took the A team -- we put them on the

00:17:12.889 --> 00:17:14.069
project.

00:17:14.089 --> 00:17:17.700
We went down to Disney and said, "The film isn't good

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:18.720
enough.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:21.869
We have to throw it away and start all over again."

00:17:21.889 --> 00:17:24.639
And the answer was, "Well, actually, it's better than you

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:25.180
think.

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:28.910
We think it is good enough, but more importantly, it's too

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.339
late.

00:17:30.359 --> 00:17:34.000
You literally do not have the time between now and then to

00:17:34.019 --> 00:17:36.410
deliver it to redo this film."

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:40.349
So we said, "It's not good enough, and we know we don't have

00:17:40.369 --> 00:17:44.160
enough time, but we're going to do it anyway."

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:45.180
So we came back.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:48.829
John told the story crew to take a good rest over the

00:17:48.849 --> 00:17:50.649
holidays and come back on January second.

00:17:50.669 --> 00:17:52.639
We were reboarding the movie.

00:17:52.659 --> 00:17:58.079
So we then started -- we now had eight months left.

00:17:58.099 --> 00:18:05.220
We then started this incredibly intense effort to get this

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:06.240
movie out.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.000
It was boarded quickly.

00:18:08.019 --> 00:18:09.519
It was pitched to the company.

00:18:09.349 --> 00:18:11.319
It was an electrifying pitch.

00:18:11.339 --> 00:18:13.690
We had a lot of overachieving people working for

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:17.660
overachieving managers to get the movie out -- worked brutal

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:20.769
hours with this.

00:18:20.789 --> 00:18:23.789
When I say 'brutal,' we had a number of people that were

00:18:23.009 --> 00:18:24.009
injured with RSI.

00:18:24.009 --> 00:18:27.579
One of them permanently left the field.

00:18:27.599 --> 00:18:31.049
We had, actually, a married couple that worked there -- and

00:18:31.069 --> 00:18:34.579
this was in June, so it's summer.

00:18:34.599 --> 00:18:39.269
And the father was supposed to drop the baby off at daycare,

00:18:39.289 --> 00:18:42.019
but forgot -- don't know why.

00:18:42.039 --> 00:18:45.539
But he came and left the baby in the car and came into work.

00:18:45.549 --> 00:18:50.250
And given, you know, as the heat was rising, the mother

00:18:50.269 --> 00:18:52.769
asked about -- and they realized -- they rushed out, and the

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:54.029
baby was unconscious.

00:18:54.049 --> 00:18:57.049
The right thing was then, they put the ice water on the

00:18:55.809 --> 00:18:56.809
baby.

00:18:56.809 --> 00:19:00.309
And the baby ended up being fine in the end, but it was one

00:19:00.309 --> 00:19:02.559
of those traumatic things like, Why did this happen?

00:19:02.559 --> 00:19:03.809
Are we working too hard?

00:19:03.809 --> 00:19:08.809
So when I say it's 'intense,' I mean it really was intense.

00:19:08.829 --> 00:19:13.599
So, but the film was finished on time, and it was a huge

00:19:13.619 --> 00:19:14.720
success.

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:19.220
Many people think that it was better than the first one.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:21.200
But we learned several things from that.

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:25.450
One, I should say on the activity of working so hard, we

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.829
adopted new ways to the way we work in terms of the RSI.

00:19:28.849 --> 00:19:32.190
We hired a full-time organimist and a dock nurse coming in

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:33.529
twice a week.

00:19:33.549 --> 00:19:37.869
A masseuse is available all the time.

00:19:37.889 --> 00:19:40.559
We limit the number of hours that people can work.

00:19:40.579 --> 00:19:43.549
There's a whole number of things that we instituted and

00:19:43.569 --> 00:19:45.389
really got people to take this seriously.

00:19:45.409 --> 00:19:49.049
A pilates and yoga classes, soccer -- everything -- just to

00:19:49.069 --> 00:19:50.069
keep people physically fit.

00:19:50.069 --> 00:19:52.319
They realize they're in for the long haul.

00:19:52.069 --> 00:19:55.569
The reason I say this, though is 'cause it's kind of a side

00:19:55.319 --> 00:19:58.069
note, but it turns out that this program costs money, which

00:19:58.069 --> 00:20:01.319
of course, we're going to do anyway, but what we found after

00:20:01.069 --> 00:20:04.109
we did this was that the injury rate dropped dramatically.

00:20:04.129 --> 00:20:08.129
The insurance company lowered our premiums and the reduction

00:20:08.149 --> 00:20:10.899
of premiums more than paid for the entire cost of the

00:20:10.789 --> 00:20:11.789
program.

00:20:11.789 --> 00:20:14.789
I say that because some people say they can't afford this

00:20:13.889 --> 00:20:14.889
kind of stuff.

00:20:14.889 --> 00:20:17.889
And I think the answer is they can be affordable and should

00:20:17.549 --> 00:20:18.549
be.

00:20:18.549 --> 00:20:23.009
On the issue of the film itself, it wasn't working.

00:20:23.029 --> 00:20:25.029
But what was it that wasn't working?

00:20:24.619 --> 00:20:27.369
Because it turns out, the theme of the story was actually

00:20:26.869 --> 00:20:27.869
the same.

00:20:27.869 --> 00:20:31.220
It was about a toy that gets stolen by the Toy Collector,

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:33.109
and ultimately, has to make this choice.

00:20:33.129 --> 00:20:36.889
It wasn't working as a movie.

00:20:36.909 --> 00:20:41.230
And the reason was that the movie's coming out from Pixar

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:44.500
and Disney, so you know that Woody's going to end up with

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:45.000
his original family.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:48.500
If you know what the end is before you start, you don't have

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:49.159
a movie.

00:20:49.179 --> 00:20:53.609
So you need to have people believe that he has a real choice

00:20:53.629 --> 00:20:54.710
to make.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:57.730
And so, when they came back and reboarded it, that's when

00:20:57.460 --> 00:21:02.289
they add Jessie's Song, who talked about her growing up and

00:21:02.309 --> 00:21:06.980
she was the darling of this little girl, but the little girl

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:09.319
grew up and moved on.

00:21:09.339 --> 00:21:12.019
And that's really what happens.

00:21:12.039 --> 00:21:13.859
And that was her message to Woody.

00:21:13.879 --> 00:21:17.619
She says, "Yes, you may like the situation you were in, but

00:21:17.639 --> 00:21:19.200
life goes on.

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:20.889
It changes.

00:21:20.909 --> 00:21:23.109
You can't hang on to that."

00:21:23.129 --> 00:21:25.589
And that's something that's true, right?

00:21:25.609 --> 00:21:27.730
We all know that.

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:31.210
And because of that, we can relate to why there's a hard

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:33.399
decision that has to be made.

00:21:33.419 --> 00:21:37.180
And so, the things that are put in the movie are to get it

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:40.450
so that it relates to decisions that we all have seen or we

00:21:40.079 --> 00:21:43.549
can appreciate and that we can therefore enjoy as a movie.

00:21:43.569 --> 00:21:47.059
And that's what turns it into a real movie instead of just

00:21:47.079 --> 00:21:50.460
some cheap follow-on.

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:55.079
So, I come back to the first question, Which is more

00:21:55.099 --> 00:21:56.339
important?

00:21:56.359 --> 00:21:59.589
The, What's the central problem, finding good ideas or

00:21:59.609 --> 00:22:01.399
finding good people?

00:22:01.419 --> 00:22:05.899
And the answer is very clear: The idea was the same.

00:22:05.919 --> 00:22:09.169
If you have a good idea and you give it to mediocre group,

00:22:09.049 --> 00:22:11.339
they'll screw it up.

00:22:11.359 --> 00:22:14.970
If you give a mediocre idea to a good group, they'll fix it,

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:18.279
or they'll throw it away and come up with something else.

00:22:18.299 --> 00:22:21.809
The other thing to note is, there is a confusion, I think,

00:22:21.829 --> 00:22:25.909
that the people have and is -- the books and press kind of

00:22:25.929 --> 00:22:29.059
work this way in that we think about 'an idea.' When we

00:22:29.079 --> 00:22:31.579
think of ideas for movies, we think about ideas for

00:22:31.559 --> 00:22:32.559
products.

00:22:32.559 --> 00:22:35.389
And it's usually thought of as some singular thing.

00:22:35.409 --> 00:22:38.190
But the reality is, these successful movies -- as with all

00:22:38.210 --> 00:22:41.210
successful products -- have got thousands of ideas.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:44.230
It's just all sorts of things necessary to make it be

00:22:43.849 --> 00:22:45.069
successful.

00:22:45.089 --> 00:22:48.799
And you have to get most of them right to do it.

00:22:48.819 --> 00:22:51.909
And that's why you need a team that works well together.

00:22:51.929 --> 00:22:59.309
Now, we had a development department at the time, and our

00:22:59.329 --> 00:23:01.579
development department was structured just as it was with

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:02.480
other studios.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:07.980
That is, it's a group of people looking for ideas to make

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:09.579
into movies.

00:23:09.599 --> 00:23:12.960
And as we went through this, we realized, "We're thinking

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:15.389
about it in the wrong way."

00:23:15.409 --> 00:23:19.779
The goal of development is not to find good ideas.

00:23:19.799 --> 00:23:22.930
It's to put together teams of people that function well

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:24.149
together.

00:23:24.169 --> 00:23:27.190
And that change just altered the way we thought about making

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:28.450
movies.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:31.220
And to this day, this development department is loved by the

00:23:31.099 --> 00:23:32.849
directors, because it's a support group.

00:23:32.639 --> 00:23:34.889
It's not somebody telling them what to do.

00:23:34.839 --> 00:23:36.409
It's not a gate.

00:23:36.429 --> 00:23:37.849
It's a support group.

00:23:37.869 --> 00:23:41.079
The directors -- because we're a director-driven studio --

00:23:41.099 --> 00:23:45.019
are the ones who are responsible for the movie and

00:23:45.039 --> 00:23:47.019
everything else is to support them.

00:23:47.039 --> 00:23:48.869
On the way, we measure progress.

00:23:48.889 --> 00:23:50.899
It's how well that team gets together.

00:23:50.919 --> 00:23:54.659
Because initially, the films they put together -- they're a

00:23:54.679 --> 00:23:55.849
mess.

00:23:55.869 --> 00:23:57.619
It's like everything else in life.

00:23:57.069 --> 00:23:59.619
The first time you do it, it's a mess.

00:23:59.639 --> 00:24:02.639
And sometimes it's labeled, "Well, the first time it's a

00:24:02.349 --> 00:24:05.349
failure," but it's not even the right word to use, right?

00:24:05.099 --> 00:24:08.599
It's just like you get the first one out, you'll learn from

00:24:07.349 --> 00:24:08.349
it.

00:24:08.349 --> 00:24:11.599
And the only failure is, if you don't learn from it -- if

00:24:11.099 --> 00:24:12.099
you don't progress.

00:24:12.099 --> 00:24:15.099
So the way we measure it is like, "Is this team functioning

00:24:15.099 --> 00:24:18.349
well together?" And it's the thing that's never let us down.

00:24:17.849 --> 00:24:19.849
When that team functions well, they will succeed.

00:24:19.849 --> 00:24:24.950
When things are going wrong, they will fail.

00:24:24.970 --> 00:24:28.599
So for me, Toy Story II was the defining moment for the

00:24:28.619 --> 00:24:29.619
studio.

00:24:29.619 --> 00:24:32.009
Our third movie was our defining moment.

00:24:32.029 --> 00:24:35.750
It set the way we think about things and still do.

00:24:35.769 --> 00:24:42.039
You know, around this time, we were successful -- a lot of

00:24:42.059 --> 00:24:43.559
people were jumping on the bandwagon.

00:24:43.379 --> 00:24:48.379
One of the unfortunate consequences is that 2D animation in

00:24:48.399 --> 00:24:50.079
this country was actually shut down.

00:24:50.099 --> 00:24:52.849
They thought the audience lost the taste for 2D, and they

00:24:52.309 --> 00:24:53.309
just wanted 3D.

00:24:53.309 --> 00:24:55.000
It was all nonsense.

00:24:55.019 --> 00:24:57.019
It had nothing to do with the technology.

00:24:57.019 --> 00:24:59.700
It had to do with the story.

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:03.379
But, unfortunately, people like to copy the wrong things.

00:25:03.399 --> 00:25:06.490
Now, people do copy things.

00:25:06.509 --> 00:25:10.129
And it's the bizarrest thing, because copying, in some ways,

00:25:10.149 --> 00:25:11.960
is a form of learning.

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:14.980
So you say, "Well, I'll learn from what everybody else did.

00:25:14.659 --> 00:25:16.159
And then, I'll copy that."

00:25:15.909 --> 00:25:17.659
But, in many respects, it's shallow.

00:25:17.409 --> 00:25:20.419
It's sort of like, if you think about movies, you know,

00:25:20.439 --> 00:25:24.139
sometimes you see people do a remake of a movie.

00:25:24.159 --> 00:25:27.230
But you notice, they always remake good movies.

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:29.730
And rarely do they beat the good movies.

00:25:29.750 --> 00:25:32.669
But the fact is, there are thousands of movies out there

00:25:32.689 --> 00:25:35.189
that are actually 'great idea,' but they're poorly executed.

00:25:34.990 --> 00:25:38.559
They should be remaking bad movies.

00:25:38.579 --> 00:25:40.079
Or does it happen with product?

00:25:39.589 --> 00:25:42.589
The ones that do better aren't those that just copy somebody

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:42.490
else's good product.

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:45.240
They actually take the thing that's going wrong and fix

00:25:45.250 --> 00:25:46.250
that.

00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:47.250
All right.

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:49.679
That's the better idea.

00:25:49.699 --> 00:25:52.789
So anyway, what we found was, all of a sudden, everybody is

00:25:52.809 --> 00:25:54.559
trying to copy what we're doing.

00:25:54.309 --> 00:25:57.059
You know, they could copy the technology, but they couldn't

00:25:56.809 --> 00:25:59.809
copy the process we were using to come up with the story.

00:25:59.809 --> 00:26:04.960
Now, even though -- we have this great Brain Trust, though,

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:09.429
and a lot of experience and learned all this, things didn't

00:26:09.449 --> 00:26:11.450
get any easier.

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:14.720
We kind of hoped, at some point, you'd get under your belt,

00:26:14.379 --> 00:26:16.879
and it gets easier, but it didn't get easier.

00:26:16.699 --> 00:26:21.289
So we instituted a process in doing postmortems after every

00:26:21.309 --> 00:26:23.789
movie, trying to do a deep analysis.

00:26:23.809 --> 00:26:27.369
The first postmortems were very successful.

00:26:27.389 --> 00:26:30.139
We worked very hard to make sure people were safe -- they

00:26:29.579 --> 00:26:32.359
didn't get shot for pointing out problems.

00:26:32.379 --> 00:26:34.710
And everybody got a lot out of them.

00:26:34.730 --> 00:26:36.369
They valued them greatly.

00:26:36.389 --> 00:26:38.389
So we're off to a good start.

00:26:38.179 --> 00:26:42.789
But then, as you go on to the next postmortem, people began

00:26:42.809 --> 00:26:45.329
to game the system.

00:26:45.349 --> 00:26:48.299
And it turns out, people know the value -- they know they

00:26:48.319 --> 00:26:51.069
should do them -- but they don't like to do it.

00:26:50.769 --> 00:26:53.849
So why don't they like to do the postmortems?

00:26:53.869 --> 00:26:56.679
Why don't they like to do the analysis?

00:26:56.699 --> 00:26:59.449
Well, they're kind of tired of working on something for

00:26:58.699 --> 00:26:59.699
three years.

00:26:59.699 --> 00:27:02.250
They don't want to think about it anymore.

00:27:02.269 --> 00:27:05.269
Some people are defensive.

00:27:05.289 --> 00:27:08.539
Some people feel that one of the things they want to do in

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:10.250
these meetings is make their team feel good.

00:27:09.949 --> 00:27:16.200
"Look what we did." So it's not really an in-depth analysis.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:20.639
And for us then, the task is to keep pushing this, because

00:27:20.659 --> 00:27:23.659
unless we do the in-depth analysis, we will always go for

00:27:23.269 --> 00:27:24.269
the easy thing.

00:27:24.269 --> 00:27:27.269
Because again, it's when you're successful and the film goes

00:27:26.419 --> 00:27:29.419
off right, you kind of like to stop at that point -- you

00:27:29.299 --> 00:27:32.009
sort of bask in it and not dive deeper.

00:27:32.029 --> 00:27:35.529
So what we found is, we have to change the way we do the

00:27:35.519 --> 00:27:37.379
postmortems every single film.

00:27:37.399 --> 00:27:39.899
Because the next film, they'll game it.

00:27:39.919 --> 00:27:43.419
But if we can change it in the right way, then it will work.

00:27:43.039 --> 00:27:46.869
So, the latest thing we're doing until it gets gamed is, we'

00:27:46.889 --> 00:27:52.149
re asking them to pick, from their process, the five things

00:27:52.169 --> 00:27:55.240
that they would do again and the five things they wouldn't

00:27:55.259 --> 00:27:56.259
do again.

00:27:56.259 --> 00:27:59.129
We try to get both to get the balance.

00:27:59.149 --> 00:28:01.899
What we're really interested in are the things that they

00:28:01.399 --> 00:28:02.399
wouldn't do again.

00:28:02.399 --> 00:28:04.519
But you try to get both out there.

00:28:04.539 --> 00:28:07.629
And the other -- and this is a fairly important one -- is to

00:28:07.649 --> 00:28:09.869
get a lot of facts about the process.

00:28:09.889 --> 00:28:13.139
When you put the facts up, and you factor it in, it actually

00:28:12.849 --> 00:28:14.039
stimulates discussion.

00:28:14.059 --> 00:28:17.509
And it's those discussions which are very valuable.

00:28:17.529 --> 00:28:21.029
You come out of that -- we have a new theory about how to do

00:28:20.189 --> 00:28:21.189
things.

00:28:21.189 --> 00:28:24.939
And we would get it -- so we'd come up with a new way of

00:28:23.039 --> 00:28:24.039
doing it.

00:28:24.039 --> 00:28:27.289
And we'd get that every time about two-thirds right and one-

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:27.500
third wrong.

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:31.049
And you never know what the mix is going to be, but roughly,

00:28:31.069 --> 00:28:34.569
it's like, one-third of the stuff you try to do is just

00:28:33.799 --> 00:28:36.299
bogus, because you called it wrong, but that's okay.

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:39.149
At least you got two-thirds right.

00:28:39.169 --> 00:28:42.169
So let me summarize a few of the things we've learned.

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:43.809
One is the constant review.

00:28:43.829 --> 00:28:48.769
The second is, it must be safe for people to tell the truth.

00:28:48.789 --> 00:28:52.210
Third is that communication should not mirror the

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:55.439
organizational hierarchy.

00:28:55.459 --> 00:28:58.950
People and how they function is more important than ideas.

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:01.339
And do not let success mask problems.

00:29:01.359 --> 00:29:02.359
Do a deep assessment.

00:29:02.359 --> 00:29:06.329
Now, having said those things, because it's a part of the

00:29:06.349 --> 00:29:07.939
culture of Pixar.

00:29:07.959 --> 00:29:11.740
And well, there's another problem.

00:29:11.759 --> 00:29:17.019
So let me set this up by discussing another discovery.

00:29:17.039 --> 00:29:22.559
Early on, we came to believe that the story is the most

00:29:22.579 --> 00:29:25.079
important thing in our process -- it led our decision-making

00:29:24.949 --> 00:29:26.159
process.

00:29:26.179 --> 00:29:29.679
And so, as we had a lot of different polls, we'd come back

00:29:29.269 --> 00:29:35.599
to this driving principle, "It's the story that counts."

00:29:35.619 --> 00:29:38.619
And we thought this is one of the things that made us

00:29:37.679 --> 00:29:41.019
successful was this realization.

00:29:41.039 --> 00:29:44.289
And now so, having said that and we would say that and we

00:29:43.949 --> 00:29:45.199
would tell people about it.

00:29:45.199 --> 00:29:49.159
And we made all the sacrifices for it.

00:29:49.179 --> 00:29:52.000
And then, I discovered -- as I was listening around -- that

00:29:52.019 --> 00:29:55.769
every studio says the same thing.

00:29:55.789 --> 00:29:59.210
Everybody says "The story's the most important thing," even

00:29:59.230 --> 00:30:01.839
if the story was drivel.

00:30:01.859 --> 00:30:08.389
It might be true -- in fact, it is true -- but it doesn't

00:30:08.409 --> 00:30:10.750
affect behavior.

00:30:10.769 --> 00:30:13.769
Now, when I say "story is the most important thing," I think

00:30:13.669 --> 00:30:16.329
the analogy is probably "quality is the most important

00:30:16.349 --> 00:30:17.349
thing."

00:30:17.349 --> 00:30:21.099
It's one of those things that's true and you agree it's true

00:30:20.349 --> 00:30:21.349
and you say it.

00:30:21.349 --> 00:30:23.960
That doesn't mean anything.

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:28.429
Now, when I first started Pixar, I came from a pure

00:30:28.449 --> 00:30:30.579
technical background -- I had to learn about business

00:30:30.599 --> 00:30:31.599
quickly.

00:30:31.599 --> 00:30:35.099
And I hadn't gone to business school, so I just read a lot

00:30:34.659 --> 00:30:35.899
of books.

00:30:35.919 --> 00:30:39.429
And there were a few bits and pieces, but I got to say for

00:30:39.449 --> 00:30:42.699
the most part, I didn't get a lot of traction with them.

00:30:42.449 --> 00:30:44.710
Tried a variety of things.

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:48.230
So I said, "Well, maybe the problem is, I got to get to the

00:30:47.970 --> 00:30:48.970
essence of them."

00:30:48.970 --> 00:30:53.379
So there's this service that gets the summaries in business

00:30:53.399 --> 00:30:54.579
books.

00:30:54.599 --> 00:30:57.369
So I tried that, and I read those.

00:30:57.389 --> 00:30:59.809
That was actually an amazing experience, because in reading

00:30:59.829 --> 00:31:06.919
the essence of the books, I realize they were content-free.

00:31:06.939 --> 00:31:09.439
[laughter] So I thought, "Well, what's going on here?

00:31:09.089 --> 00:31:12.500
Is it the fact that the book doesn't have any content?" --

00:31:12.519 --> 00:31:18.119
which is probably true in many cases -- or, another thing is

00:31:18.139 --> 00:31:21.309
-- "Is it that you can't take some of these things and

00:31:21.329 --> 00:31:24.899
reduce them in a meaningful way?" -- which I suspect is also

00:31:24.919 --> 00:31:27.439
true.

00:31:27.459 --> 00:31:28.959
I ran into a similar thing.

00:31:28.539 --> 00:31:30.039
This just happened a while ago.

00:31:29.929 --> 00:31:32.679
It's similar to "the story is the most important thing."

00:31:32.429 --> 00:31:34.679
It comes right out of the world of architecture.

00:31:34.679 --> 00:31:41.299
And at that time, we were looking at building a building.

00:31:41.319 --> 00:31:43.599
Actually, it was just this last year.

00:31:43.619 --> 00:31:46.470
And I know there's some buildings down there which are

00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:47.490
abysmal.

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:51.529
They're just awful buildings.

00:31:51.549 --> 00:31:54.299
And so, I started talking with our building person up at

00:31:54.309 --> 00:31:57.710
Pixar as we're coordinating to get this built, he explained

00:31:57.730 --> 00:32:00.500
to me the concept in architecture, "designing from the

00:32:00.519 --> 00:32:05.129
inside out" as opposed to "from the outside in." And "

00:32:05.149 --> 00:32:08.399
outside in" is the bad one that you get the look of the

00:32:08.299 --> 00:32:10.799
building, and sort of that dictates what you're doing.

00:32:10.549 --> 00:32:12.049
You end up with poor buildings.

00:32:12.049 --> 00:32:14.549
And 'inside out' is where you think about the functions,

00:32:14.549 --> 00:32:17.299
where people work, and that sort of dictates things, and you

00:32:17.299 --> 00:32:19.549
try to make it look good from the outside.

00:32:19.549 --> 00:32:22.549
So, in that world, 'inside out' is good, and 'outside in' is

00:32:21.799 --> 00:32:22.799
bad.

00:32:22.799 --> 00:32:28.939
So, then I met with an architect who was responsible for one

00:32:28.959 --> 00:32:30.460
of the buildings.

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:33.919
And he used the phrase, "If you design the buildings from

00:32:33.939 --> 00:32:39.109
the 'inside out,'" but the building he designed was a

00:32:39.129 --> 00:32:41.289
disaster.

00:32:41.309 --> 00:32:43.809
So I realize, "Oh, it's the same thing."

00:32:43.829 --> 00:32:46.470
The phrases important in this community -- it just doesn't

00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:49.829
have any effect on behavior.

00:32:49.849 --> 00:32:55.439
So once -- and the principle is -- once one can articulate

00:32:55.459 --> 00:33:01.009
an important idea into a concise statement, then one can use

00:33:01.029 --> 00:33:03.939
the statement and not have to have the fear of changing

00:33:03.959 --> 00:33:05.710
behavior.

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:07.980
And I just see this over and over again.

00:33:07.699 --> 00:33:10.949
So it's like, I can summarize some of these things, but the

00:33:10.699 --> 00:33:13.949
real issue is, "What do we do?" Not even what we say about

00:33:13.949 --> 00:33:16.629
it, What are the things that we do?

00:33:16.649 --> 00:33:21.629
Every product that we're making has got to be original,

00:33:21.649 --> 00:33:24.919
which implies that we can never repeat ourselves.

00:33:24.939 --> 00:33:28.819
Now, this is -- to me, it's an important thing -- because a

00:33:28.839 --> 00:33:31.339
lot of people in Hollywood actually don't understand that.

00:33:31.089 --> 00:33:34.879
If something is successful -- if you've got Star Wars or

00:33:34.899 --> 00:33:37.889
Shrek or whatever, then basically, "Let's do that again,"

00:33:37.909 --> 00:33:38.909
right?

00:33:38.909 --> 00:33:41.899
We'll try to do the same thing again.

00:33:41.919 --> 00:33:44.629
But you can't think that way with any products.

00:33:44.649 --> 00:33:45.899
Everything is new and original.

00:33:45.799 --> 00:33:48.299
And therefore, our way of dealing with and solving the

00:33:48.069 --> 00:33:50.519
problems has got to be an original.

00:33:50.539 --> 00:33:53.539
So the secret is, we have to keep on digging deeper and

00:33:53.159 --> 00:33:55.659
deeper and knowing that we're always missing something that'

00:33:55.029 --> 00:33:56.029
s important.

00:33:56.029 --> 00:33:59.819
So let me return to the first question.

00:33:59.839 --> 00:34:01.589
Why do successful companies fail?

00:34:01.609 --> 00:34:06.049
For that matter, why did Golden Age is in?

00:34:06.069 --> 00:34:08.730
I do believe that organizations -- human organizations --

00:34:08.750 --> 00:34:11.710
are inherently unstable.

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:15.090
They will fall over, and you have to work to keep them

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:16.110
upright.

00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:18.309
But they fall slowly.

00:34:18.329 --> 00:34:20.969
Most people don't notice it.

00:34:20.989 --> 00:34:24.610
They let their success blind them.

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:26.630
They don't see it's falling over.

00:34:26.179 --> 00:34:30.650
The falling takes place slow, but the collapse is quick.

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:33.889
You have to do constant assessment.

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:36.389
You have to look for the hard truths.

00:34:36.409 --> 00:34:38.789
Mentioned Cassandra before -- it's the Greek myth.

00:34:38.809 --> 00:34:41.559
And again, she's cursed with seeing the truth, but nobody

00:34:41.539 --> 00:34:42.539
will believe her.

00:34:42.539 --> 00:34:45.039
I don't think the Greeks had it right, actually.

00:34:44.789 --> 00:34:49.659
I think the curse is on the people that don't listen, right?

00:34:49.679 --> 00:34:53.179
We have to be the ones who are looking for it all the time.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:57.079
And when we hear things evaluate it, "Is it right?" I think

00:34:57.099 --> 00:35:01.420
in the movies -- in our process -- we have various crises,

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:04.969
but there are two fundamental kinds of crises.

00:35:04.989 --> 00:35:08.469
One is that you don't like what you see.

00:35:08.489 --> 00:35:10.509
So you have to make changes.

00:35:10.529 --> 00:35:13.849
Now, the fact that you make changes is not in and of itself

00:35:13.869 --> 00:35:14.869
a crisis.

00:35:14.869 --> 00:35:17.079
That's just hard work.

00:35:17.099 --> 00:35:20.349
What makes it crisis is that there are a lot of people with

00:35:20.319 --> 00:35:21.639
vested interests and with positions.

00:35:21.659 --> 00:35:25.210
And you've got to actually rearrange things, and it's people

00:35:25.230 --> 00:35:26.670
butting up against each other.

00:35:26.690 --> 00:35:28.690
And that's a hard thing to do.

00:35:28.509 --> 00:35:30.259
It's an emotional thing to do.

00:35:29.989 --> 00:35:34.940
But you do that, and you end up making a better film.

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:38.750
The second kind of crisis is, you release the film, and the

00:35:38.769 --> 00:35:40.019
audience doesn't like it.

00:35:39.809 --> 00:35:45.549
Now, at that point, it's too late to do anything about it.

00:35:45.569 --> 00:35:49.150
But the difference between the two crises is that the first

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:51.980
one is self-imposed.

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:53.500
And that's the important part.

00:35:53.159 --> 00:35:57.219
Now -- and in this talk, I've focused on our problems, and

00:35:57.239 --> 00:36:02.759
the tone might be a little downbeat on some of this, but I

00:36:02.779 --> 00:36:04.639
haven't talked about the fun part.

00:36:04.659 --> 00:36:07.380
But this approach in thinking about it and always diving

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:10.449
deeper and questioning, has led us to assemble the best

00:36:10.469 --> 00:36:11.469
people in the industry.

00:36:11.469 --> 00:36:15.230
It's the most extraordinary team I've worked with.

00:36:15.250 --> 00:36:19.110
We are in this extraordinary building up at Pixar that it's

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:21.279
one of the greatest places to work.

00:36:21.299 --> 00:36:24.299
We have a lot of fun together and a lot of laughter.

00:36:24.219 --> 00:36:26.219
And our job is to keep it going.

00:36:25.719 --> 00:36:26.719
Thank you.

00:36:26.719 --> 00:36:26.919
&gt;&gt;

00:36:26.719 --> 00:36:27.719
[Applause]

00:36:26.719 --> 00:36:39.519
&gt;&gt; Take some questions?

00:36:39.539 --> 00:36:47.920
Q You mentioned that Silicon Graphics made some crucial

00:36:47.940 --> 00:36:48.940
mistakes, what were they?

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:54.690
A In my opinion was, they bought Cray Computer, all right?

00:36:54.710 --> 00:37:01.610
The second one was, they came out with an add-in PC board,

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:04.019
and it was not successful.

00:37:04.039 --> 00:37:07.039
They reached the conclusion that it was not a good idea, all

00:37:06.549 --> 00:37:07.549
right?

00:37:07.549 --> 00:37:10.049
Rather than saying, "The first one is not successful, what

00:37:09.949 --> 00:37:12.250
should we do to make it successful?" Because that,

00:37:12.269 --> 00:37:16.339
basically, is the way the whole industry is structured now.

00:37:16.359 --> 00:37:19.109
And it's not taking the information they learned from that

00:37:18.969 --> 00:37:22.190
first failure, if you will, and turning it into a success.

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:25.809
Q How do you document this?

00:37:25.829 --> 00:37:28.829
And how do you make sure that this knowledge that you're

00:37:28.579 --> 00:37:30.329
gaining is actually turning into something practical?

00:37:30.329 --> 00:37:34.259
Was there somebody documenting this and writing down the

00:37:34.279 --> 00:37:37.269
process to work on, or how would you do it?

00:37:37.289 --> 00:37:39.789
A Honestly, in all of our meetings -- whether creative or

00:37:39.659 --> 00:37:41.659
postmortem -- we have notetakers.

00:37:41.679 --> 00:37:44.849
And they're staff members, but they're really good.

00:37:44.869 --> 00:37:47.519
They just keep track of everything.

00:37:47.539 --> 00:37:52.259
The second thing is that the people -- in preparing for it -

00:37:52.279 --> 00:37:53.779
- actually get a lot of information.

00:37:53.779 --> 00:37:56.779
It's the people in charge who go through the process and,

00:37:56.609 --> 00:38:00.069
thinking through it, get a lot of their information.

00:38:00.089 --> 00:38:02.839
And the people who are then presented to are frequently the

00:38:02.859 --> 00:38:05.409
leaders of a follow-on film.

00:38:05.429 --> 00:38:08.069
So there's a direct hand-off in communication.

00:38:08.089 --> 00:38:11.960
And just having the discussion surfaces a lot of issues.

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:15.719
So there's a variety of things.

00:38:15.739 --> 00:38:18.739
Excuses why they don't do it are frequently like, "Well, I'

00:38:18.159 --> 00:38:20.909
ve already started the next process, so it's too late.

00:38:20.659 --> 00:38:22.409
The postmortem happens too late for me."

00:38:22.089 --> 00:38:25.089
So there are things like that, which get in the way, in

00:38:24.869 --> 00:38:26.869
addition to people not wanting to do it.

00:38:26.869 --> 00:38:30.539
So for me, it's saying, "Okay, we all know it's good, but

00:38:30.559 --> 00:38:32.309
it's like your medicine, you know?

00:38:32.039 --> 00:38:33.539
You've got to do it.

00:38:33.409 --> 00:38:36.089
Or actually, like your vitamins -- it's something you take.

00:38:36.109 --> 00:38:38.009
You need to do it."

00:38:38.029 --> 00:38:39.029
Yes.

00:38:39.029 --> 00:38:41.529
Q You talked about collecting facts, so how effective or

00:38:41.329 --> 00:38:42.329
ineffective

00:38:42.329 --> 00:38:43.329
[inaudible]

00:38:42.329 --> 00:39:00.489
A The process of gathering the evidence is mixed.

00:39:00.509 --> 00:39:02.710
That is, we're fairly good at it.

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:05.980
And we're getting better at it, but there are a number of

00:39:05.599 --> 00:39:08.769
times you haven't kept track of the detail.

00:39:08.789 --> 00:39:11.539
In general, when we actually gather the facts and we present

00:39:11.549 --> 00:39:15.199
it, then the reaction of most people is like they're just

00:39:15.219 --> 00:39:16.789
fascinated by it.

00:39:16.809 --> 00:39:19.909
Because they're flying by their gut, which is pretty good,

00:39:19.929 --> 00:39:22.429
but there are things they missed that the facts show.

00:39:22.309 --> 00:39:29.000
And people don't let facts override judgment, but it

00:39:29.019 --> 00:39:31.769
actually is -- it sometimes gives you pictures you can't see

00:39:31.750 --> 00:39:32.750
otherwise.

00:39:32.750 --> 00:39:37.920
So it's very well-appreciated at Pixar.

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:40.940
And we've got -- in fact, there's a graduate from Stanford

00:39:40.699 --> 00:39:43.460
University, Alori Organi, who's brilliant.

00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:49.569
And he was extremely good at taking facts and teasing out

00:39:49.589 --> 00:39:51.170
important information.

00:39:51.190 --> 00:39:53.489
And you present that important information in really concise

00:39:53.509 --> 00:39:56.509
ways, and just -- it's things that people didn't see before.

00:39:56.449 --> 00:39:59.199
So the fact they see that means they want to participate,

00:39:58.799 --> 00:40:03.779
and it helps them commit to gathering facts in the future.

00:40:03.799 --> 00:40:06.690
So it's a very successful way of working.

00:40:06.710 --> 00:40:07.710
Yes.

00:40:07.710 --> 00:40:16.559
Q So second question that you raised, finding good people

00:40:16.579 --> 00:40:17.579
vs. good ideas.

00:40:17.579 --> 00:40:19.829
I think they kind of go hand in hand.

00:40:19.829 --> 00:40:23.159
So have you really reached the conclusion that they really

00:40:23.179 --> 00:40:25.679
should go together as opposed to one vs. the other?

00:40:25.679 --> 00:40:27.429
A Where do the ideas come from?

00:40:27.429 --> 00:40:30.610
The reason for phrasing it that way -- because you

00:40:30.630 --> 00:40:34.489
essentially need the new and the fresh ideas -- is, what

00:40:34.509 --> 00:40:40.110
you're doing is giving credit and camaraderie to a team of

00:40:40.130 --> 00:40:41.289
people.

00:40:41.309 --> 00:40:43.309
You can't give that to an idea.

00:40:43.069 --> 00:40:46.319
And what you're trying to do is, have the people work well

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:46.809
together.

00:40:46.829 --> 00:40:49.980
So I'd much rather say, "The people are the important thing,

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:52.500
and they're going to come up with the ideas."

00:40:52.250 --> 00:40:54.500
If I say, "The ideas are the important thing."

00:40:54.500 --> 00:40:57.750
It's like, "Okay, what do you want to come up with?" You

00:40:57.500 --> 00:40:59.000
know, you have to support them.

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:02.349
So that's why I do put the people first, because the ideas

00:41:02.369 --> 00:41:04.750
are a process that result from people working together.

00:41:04.769 --> 00:41:08.139
I mean, the result of people working well together.

00:41:08.159 --> 00:41:10.049
Yes.

00:41:10.069 --> 00:41:11.069
Q Two quick things.

00:41:11.069 --> 00:41:13.819
First, is there any way to share the Pixar process with

00:41:13.819 --> 00:41:15.819
becoming on stage as far as right now?

00:41:15.819 --> 00:41:21.960
Is the ability used an effective thing is, How do you track

00:41:21.980 --> 00:41:22.980
audience?

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:26.009
How do you segment and track audience interests, and is that

00:41:26.029 --> 00:41:29.599
something that changed and evolved over time as you're doing

00:41:29.619 --> 00:41:30.619
these films?

00:41:30.619 --> 00:41:37.659
A Well, on the first question -- oh, God, I wish -- no,

00:41:37.679 --> 00:41:41.630
actually, I do believe there is a notion of self-assessment,

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:47.569
which is unfortunately lacking.

00:41:47.589 --> 00:41:51.089
I think one of the things, if I look at the left and the

00:41:50.659 --> 00:41:54.819
right, fundamentally, they actually don't trust people.

00:41:54.839 --> 00:41:58.339
The thing I like about the center is that it's more based on

00:41:58.210 --> 00:42:00.360
trust, not that mistakes aren't made.

00:42:00.380 --> 00:42:03.630
When I look at the extremes, it's like, "We're going to

00:42:03.130 --> 00:42:05.710
impose something," and it's not a trusting mechanism.

00:42:05.730 --> 00:42:11.079
And I think the notion of self-assessment is apolitical.

00:42:11.099 --> 00:42:14.759
It doesn't matter whether Republican or Democrat.

00:42:14.779 --> 00:42:17.279
Some people sort of get the notion of 'honest assessment,'

00:42:17.299 --> 00:42:20.299
and some people don't, because they've got an agenda -- you

00:42:19.799 --> 00:42:23.509
know -- and we all know that.

00:42:23.529 --> 00:42:25.469
And we see the results.

00:42:25.489 --> 00:42:29.589
And we talk about -- so when the golden age is in, even at

00:42:29.609 --> 00:42:30.609
the national level.

00:42:30.609 --> 00:42:33.559
Are we guaranteed that America will be great in 200 years?

00:42:33.579 --> 00:42:34.579
There are no guarantees.

00:42:34.579 --> 00:42:37.110
We have no guarantees any company will be around.

00:42:37.130 --> 00:42:40.849
You know, all of it requires constant diligence in the

00:42:40.869 --> 00:42:43.940
thought process, which is to say, we have responsibility to

00:42:43.960 --> 00:42:46.869
make them better.

00:42:46.889 --> 00:42:56.489
As far as heading for an audience, we don't target a group -

00:42:56.509 --> 00:43:00.019
- in particular, we don't go after kids, all right.

00:43:00.039 --> 00:43:02.869
It's a bad idea to target films for little kids.

00:43:02.889 --> 00:43:04.069
Two reasons.

00:43:04.089 --> 00:43:06.829
One is, it essentially is talking down to them.

00:43:06.849 --> 00:43:11.029
And if you talk down to them, they don't like it that much.

00:43:11.049 --> 00:43:12.880
And for adults, it's very boring.

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.900
The truth is, children live in an adult world, and they're

00:43:15.819 --> 00:43:18.089
used to hearing things they don't understand.

00:43:18.109 --> 00:43:21.109
So our approach is, we want to make movies that we enjoy.

00:43:21.019 --> 00:43:25.319
There's a physical humor and physical comedy that comes from

00:43:25.339 --> 00:43:27.789
acting from animation that kids do enjoy.

00:43:27.809 --> 00:43:30.289
So there's a thing that they do like.

00:43:30.309 --> 00:43:34.069
And obviously, we don't put in things into our movies that

00:43:34.089 --> 00:43:37.889
would offend families or their children.

00:43:37.909 --> 00:43:41.679
But, we write the dialogue and the story for us.

00:43:41.699 --> 00:43:43.699
We want them to be meaningful to us.

00:43:43.359 --> 00:43:46.599
And that's how we think about it.

00:43:46.619 --> 00:43:49.369
We will do some late screenings after the story is fairly

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:49.750
well-along.

00:43:49.750 --> 00:43:53.000
Partly because, if you've seen a movie over and over again,

00:43:53.019 --> 00:43:54.519
you actually lose perspective on it.

00:43:54.519 --> 00:43:57.519
So sometimes you just need that somebody who's never seen it

00:43:57.269 --> 00:44:00.049
before and you just sit there in the room.

00:44:00.069 --> 00:44:02.069
And it sort of opens up your eyes.

00:44:01.949 --> 00:44:03.449
You don't even record anything.

00:44:03.199 --> 00:44:05.989
You just see things that are just different.

00:44:06.009 --> 00:44:09.210
And that's more of the way that the feedback works.

00:44:09.230 --> 00:44:10.230
Yes.

00:44:10.230 --> 00:44:11.230
Q

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:19.170
[inaudible] evolution company writerband to a service

00:44:19.190 --> 00:44:20.190
system.

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:23.190
It looks like a brilliant move now, but I'm wondering ten

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:26.289
years ago, was it -- when you try to transform a company and

00:44:26.309 --> 00:44:29.309
the culture, was it as obvious a decision as it turned to

00:44:28.559 --> 00:44:29.559
be?

00:44:29.559 --> 00:44:32.559
A Well, when we formed the company, we spun off from Lucas

00:44:31.809 --> 00:44:32.809
Film.

00:44:32.809 --> 00:44:35.309
And initially, we sold the hardware product, which is an

00:44:34.809 --> 00:44:35.809
image computer.

00:44:35.809 --> 00:44:41.259
And that was rough.

00:44:41.279 --> 00:44:43.029
That's my reading all the books.

00:44:42.719 --> 00:44:49.690
And I called Jim Clark, the head of Sun -- Scott Bernelli --

00:44:49.710 --> 00:44:50.710
for advice.

00:44:50.710 --> 00:44:53.710
And it turns out that well-meaning advice, but it turns out,

00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:56.210
it was the wrong advice, because it wasn't appropriate for

00:44:55.960 --> 00:44:56.960
what we were doing.

00:44:56.960 --> 00:45:00.880
I didn't know enough to sort of figure some of this out.

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:04.779
And we were -- it was hard -- and we got into the software

00:45:04.799 --> 00:45:07.199
business.

00:45:07.219 --> 00:45:11.210
We then did the RenderMan software, and we started doing

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:12.230
commercials.

00:45:12.230 --> 00:45:14.730
But I got to tell you, we kept losing money.

00:45:14.719 --> 00:45:16.469
It didn't matter what we did.

00:45:16.339 --> 00:45:20.799
The one nice thing was, is that, our investor was Steve

00:45:20.819 --> 00:45:21.949
Jobs.

00:45:21.969 --> 00:45:29.250
And now mind you, in one sense Steve is -- he's not known

00:45:29.269 --> 00:45:33.670
for being patient -- but the reality is that he was patient

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:35.250
with us.

00:45:35.269 --> 00:45:37.089
He was funding our education.

00:45:37.109 --> 00:45:40.359
And so, by the time they got the chance for the big one,

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:42.779
which was the movie, we'd been through a lot.

00:45:42.799 --> 00:45:45.049
We had bonded together as a team.

00:45:45.069 --> 00:45:49.009
And he funded that education, but then it came back in

00:45:49.029 --> 00:45:52.460
spades, because we worked together with him as a team to

00:45:52.480 --> 00:45:54.139
build this rather remarkable place.

00:45:54.159 --> 00:45:58.319
And it was really cool, really, to have an investor sort of

00:45:58.339 --> 00:46:02.049
understood what we were doing and what we were going for,

00:46:02.069 --> 00:46:05.750
which really helped change the industry.

00:46:05.769 --> 00:46:07.989
So, we moved over into filmmaking.

00:46:08.009 --> 00:46:11.460
So we're actually not in service anymore, except for, we do

00:46:11.480 --> 00:46:12.869
still sell RenderMan.

00:46:12.889 --> 00:46:15.609
So it's just really nice business.

00:46:15.629 --> 00:46:19.049
It's -- almost all the special effects you see in movies are

00:46:19.069 --> 00:46:20.569
using the software that we sell.

00:46:20.559 --> 00:46:26.489
It's the only piece of software that we do sell.

00:46:26.509 --> 00:46:29.349
We don't sell any of our other animation software, because

00:46:29.369 --> 00:46:31.619
we think there's a certain advantage we have.

00:46:31.179 --> 00:46:34.179
But in this case, we like the fact that there's an

00:46:33.929 --> 00:46:36.940
industrywide standard.

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:37.960
Yes.

00:46:37.319 --> 00:46:38.319
Q

00:46:37.569 --> 00:46:39.569
[inaudible] Successful process of telling stories, that back

00:46:39.319 --> 00:46:46.750
then you worked with animated actors instead of real, live

00:46:46.769 --> 00:46:47.769
actors.

00:46:47.769 --> 00:46:51.679
And where do you see the industry heading?

00:46:51.699 --> 00:46:54.940
A Well, when we make films.

00:46:54.960 --> 00:46:58.480
The question is animated actors vs. 'live action' actors.

00:46:58.500 --> 00:47:02.559
The characters in our films are really a combination of the

00:47:02.579 --> 00:47:06.509
writers from the story team putting it together, the design,

00:47:06.529 --> 00:47:09.279
and the animators from the physical acting and the voice of

00:47:08.929 --> 00:47:09.929
the actor.

00:47:09.929 --> 00:47:13.159
Since we pick and select actors, we've got really remarkable

00:47:13.179 --> 00:47:14.179
voice.

00:47:14.179 --> 00:47:16.619
It's really that collection that creates a character.

00:47:16.639 --> 00:47:20.319
And it's just this part of the animations, which is

00:47:20.339 --> 00:47:22.329
different than live action.

00:47:22.349 --> 00:47:25.380
The time scale is different; the way that it's put together

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:26.400
is different.

00:47:26.400 --> 00:47:29.150
And in both 'live action' and 'animation', you can also do

00:47:28.559 --> 00:47:30.559
it badly and you can do it well.

00:47:30.199 --> 00:47:31.670
It just depends upon talent.

00:47:31.690 --> 00:47:32.690
Yes.

00:47:32.690 --> 00:47:46.880
Q The Pixar was a Lucas spin-off and obviously there have

00:47:46.900 --> 00:47:49.150
been other very successful Lucas spin-offs like PhotoShop

00:47:48.900 --> 00:47:50.400
and DigitalDomain and Telltale and others.

00:47:50.400 --> 00:47:52.900
What is it about Lucas veterans that makes them successful

00:47:52.150 --> 00:47:53.150
entrepreneurs?

00:47:53.150 --> 00:47:55.400
And do you see the same characteristics in Disney

00:47:54.650 --> 00:47:55.650
entrepreneurs?

00:47:55.650 --> 00:47:58.699
A Well, the -- George Lucas was really remarkable in that,

00:47:58.719 --> 00:48:01.739
having produced Star Wars, he believed that bringing Ken

00:48:01.759 --> 00:48:04.989
Alice into the industry would benefit it.

00:48:05.009 --> 00:48:10.389
And so, the job that I was given when I came in to Lucas

00:48:10.409 --> 00:48:14.389
Film in 1979, was to bring high-technology into the

00:48:14.409 --> 00:48:16.269
industry.

00:48:16.289 --> 00:48:20.670
So then, digital audio, video-editing, computer graphics and

00:48:20.690 --> 00:48:21.849
games.

00:48:21.869 --> 00:48:25.009
So nobody else was funding it in this industry except for

00:48:25.029 --> 00:48:26.029
George.

00:48:26.029 --> 00:48:29.779
So I was able to go out and hire the best people in each one

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:30.690
of these areas.

00:48:30.710 --> 00:48:33.299
So that was a great activity to be engaged with.

00:48:33.319 --> 00:48:36.339
Now, in the end, it's a filmmaking company.

00:48:36.359 --> 00:48:39.359
And we had sort of like this hotbed of ideas and good

00:48:38.609 --> 00:48:39.609
people.

00:48:39.609 --> 00:48:41.949
So there's some spin-out from that.

00:48:41.969 --> 00:48:44.389
So this was a computer division.

00:48:44.409 --> 00:48:47.599
Within ILM, they didn't actually spin out other companies.

00:48:47.619 --> 00:48:50.119
It was actually people who -- it's because they were

00:48:49.869 --> 00:48:52.670
successful that some people were stolen away to start

00:48:52.690 --> 00:48:53.690
competing companies.

00:48:53.710 --> 00:48:57.829
But it wasn't as if an island had spun them out.

00:48:57.849 --> 00:49:01.409
And in the case of PhotoShop, the original one was written

00:49:01.429 --> 00:49:05.589
by John Knolls, who is the Knolls brothers, one of whom is

00:49:05.609 --> 00:49:11.339
still there and is a su tech for making films, a rather

00:49:11.359 --> 00:49:13.069
remarkable one.

00:49:13.089 --> 00:49:15.089
But it was early sold off to Adobe.

00:49:14.639 --> 00:49:19.239
And it's big changes actually been through Adobe.

00:49:19.259 --> 00:49:22.009
But part of it is, there's something about the environment -

00:49:21.940 --> 00:49:24.980
- where you've got technology and art together -- that's a

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:26.809
great mix.

00:49:26.829 --> 00:49:28.759
It's a nice place to be.

00:49:28.779 --> 00:49:31.779
And if you think about it -- and mostly we've all thought

00:49:31.059 --> 00:49:33.559
about this -- because they think of films like the old

00:49:33.309 --> 00:49:34.309
fashion.

00:49:34.309 --> 00:49:39.119
But when Walt Disney started off back in the '30s with this,

00:49:39.139 --> 00:49:40.859
it was a technological revolution.

00:49:40.879 --> 00:49:45.159
Films were new, color was new, sound was new.

00:49:45.179 --> 00:49:47.679
He was the first one to put sound in there.

00:49:47.569 --> 00:49:49.569
He was the first one to put color.

00:49:49.569 --> 00:49:52.199
He locked up technicolor for awhile.

00:49:52.219 --> 00:49:53.969
So there were a lot of things.

00:49:53.889 --> 00:49:56.139
He was adopting every technology as it came along.

00:49:56.139 --> 00:50:00.210
And that mix of the technology on the artist provided -- or

00:50:00.230 --> 00:50:02.730
made this environment where a lot of cool things were

00:50:01.900 --> 00:50:02.900
happening.

00:50:02.900 --> 00:50:06.400
And you look back in the past, and you sort of focus on the

00:50:06.400 --> 00:50:08.650
art without realizing, "What was the environment that made

00:50:08.650 --> 00:50:11.929
that?" And then, over a while, that environment sort of went

00:50:11.949 --> 00:50:12.949
away.

00:50:12.949 --> 00:50:15.449
And then, the new environment basically picked up at Lucas

00:50:15.389 --> 00:50:18.639
Film, and then at Pixar, where there's the mix of the artist

00:50:18.359 --> 00:50:20.309
and the technical together.

00:50:20.329 --> 00:50:23.250
And now, realizing that and seeing what had happened then

00:50:23.269 --> 00:50:26.339
and what happened now, it's very important for us to keep

00:50:26.359 --> 00:50:27.750
the technology changing.

00:50:27.769 --> 00:50:31.069
Keep it mixed up.

00:50:31.089 --> 00:50:33.639
Sometimes the individuals don't always see that, when they

00:50:33.659 --> 00:50:37.159
look at it from their point of view, but you have to look at

00:50:37.159 --> 00:50:39.659
it as a whole ecosystem and having that mixture together.

00:50:39.659 --> 00:50:42.409
Sort of like what a university is supposed to be, right?

00:50:42.409 --> 00:50:45.409
A lot of things happening in one place to stimulate you to

00:50:45.409 --> 00:50:47.409
think about things in new and innovative ways.

00:50:47.159 --> 00:50:48.159
All right, good.

00:50:48.159 --> 00:50:50.409
Q Could you have successfully managed Pixar without your

00:50:50.409 --> 00:50:54.859
technical background to the success that it is today?

00:50:54.879 --> 00:50:58.289
A Well, I don't know.

00:50:58.309 --> 00:51:02.549
I always look at Pixar as really having the combination of a

00:51:02.569 --> 00:51:03.569
variety of people there.

00:51:03.569 --> 00:51:06.500
I brought one way of looking at things.

00:51:06.519 --> 00:51:07.609
John brought something.

00:51:07.629 --> 00:51:09.059
Steve Jobs brought something.

00:51:09.079 --> 00:51:11.679
And there are various other people who brought something to

00:51:11.699 --> 00:51:12.819
the mix.

00:51:12.839 --> 00:51:14.289
And it was the balance.

00:51:14.309 --> 00:51:18.559
And there are things that could have gone wrong in that, and

00:51:18.579 --> 00:51:20.079
then, that wouldn't have happened.

00:51:19.989 --> 00:51:23.489
But you know -- and none of us even point and say any of us

00:51:23.299 --> 00:51:24.299
did it alone.

00:51:24.299 --> 00:51:27.389
It really took all of us together to do that.

00:51:27.409 --> 00:51:32.179
So, I can't separate out my background and say, "Well, what

00:51:32.199 --> 00:51:35.699
would I have done different?" I am who I am, and each one of

00:51:35.699 --> 00:51:37.449
those is different in a complementary way.

00:51:37.449 --> 00:51:41.269
We're very different from each other, but that difference is

00:51:41.289 --> 00:51:44.269
our strength.

00:51:44.289 --> 00:51:45.289
Yes.

00:51:44.619 --> 00:51:49.889
Q The naive sort of assessment of selling RenderMan is that

00:51:49.909 --> 00:51:51.949
you're enabling your competition.

00:51:51.969 --> 00:51:56.319
Can you explain kind of how you look at that internally and

00:51:56.339 --> 00:51:59.089
why you continue to believe that that's the right strategy

00:51:58.839 --> 00:52:01.839
for the company, and why it helps to raise the level of

00:52:01.589 --> 00:52:02.589
excellence at Pixar?

00:52:02.589 --> 00:52:07.069
A Well, with any piece of software like that, it requires a

00:52:07.089 --> 00:52:10.519
great deal of work to keep it in good place.

00:52:10.539 --> 00:52:12.789
And we have a lot of things going on.

00:52:12.369 --> 00:52:14.869
There are certain things that we actually invented in the

00:52:14.889 --> 00:52:17.179
first place, but now, we buy commercially.

00:52:17.199 --> 00:52:19.699
When we made the first paint program, the first composited,

00:52:19.649 --> 00:52:20.649
right?

00:52:20.649 --> 00:52:22.649
But, in fact, we don't do them.

00:52:21.809 --> 00:52:25.500
We do them as commercial products.

00:52:25.519 --> 00:52:30.420
It is to our advantage to have an industrywide standard.

00:52:30.440 --> 00:52:33.190
If we didn't sell it, there would be another one.

00:52:32.909 --> 00:52:36.409
And then it would be a pain in the neck, because we would be

00:52:36.409 --> 00:52:38.909
different than the rest of the studio in something that

00:52:38.789 --> 00:52:43.420
needs to be 'across the studio' and now 'across two studios.

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:46.319
' So in that case, we're better off having an industrywide

00:52:46.339 --> 00:52:49.539
standard.

00:52:49.559 --> 00:52:52.059
For me, the technology is part of the culture.

00:52:52.079 --> 00:52:56.190
It is a -- we have some competitive advantage in several of

00:52:56.210 --> 00:53:00.440
the things we do with technology, but I do think that the

00:53:00.460 --> 00:53:03.369
biggest competitive advantage is the way we work -- we have

00:53:03.389 --> 00:53:05.440
the technology and the creative work together.

00:53:05.460 --> 00:53:08.379
The things that have been talked about is actually the thing

00:53:08.399 --> 00:53:11.149
that, if somebody hears it, they'll probably copy the wrong

00:53:10.569 --> 00:53:11.569
piece of it.

00:53:11.569 --> 00:53:14.400
So like, I can tell them what's important.

00:53:14.420 --> 00:53:15.920
It won't matter, you know?

00:53:15.670 --> 00:53:18.009
They'll just say, "Oh, I understand that.

00:53:18.029 --> 00:53:20.900
The story is the most important thing."

00:53:20.920 --> 00:53:22.819
And that, in fact, is interesting.

00:53:22.839 --> 00:53:25.589
If somebody else figures it out, then they'll make good

00:53:25.119 --> 00:53:26.119
stories.

00:53:26.119 --> 00:53:32.079
We're also in a funny industry where the competitors are

00:53:32.099 --> 00:53:35.250
competitors for the weeks that you're out, and the rest of

00:53:35.269 --> 00:53:37.519
the year, you actually want them to do well.

00:53:37.519 --> 00:53:40.420
If people go to movies and have a bad experience -- a

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:44.219
competitor's movie, have a bad experience -- then that sort

00:53:44.239 --> 00:53:47.299
of takes away their enjoyment from going to the movies.

00:53:47.319 --> 00:53:50.569
If they have a lot of bad experiences, they won't go to

00:53:49.679 --> 00:53:50.679
movies.

00:53:50.679 --> 00:53:54.179
If they go and have a great time, then they'll want to go

00:53:52.809 --> 00:53:53.809
more.

00:53:53.809 --> 00:53:58.069
So we want our competitors to do well, except when our

00:53:58.089 --> 00:54:01.440
movie's out.

00:54:01.460 --> 00:54:04.339
[pause] Then they can burn and die.

00:54:04.359 --> 00:54:05.839
[laughter] Thank you.

00:54:05.859 --> 00:54:06.059
&gt;&gt;

00:54:04.859 --> 00:54:05.859
[Applause]

